%description:

RFC 6298 -- no round-trip sample is taken from a retransmitted segment.

Checks RFC6298-KARN-1 (must not).

RFC 6298 section 3, Karn's rule: a round-trip sample is never taken from a segment that was
retransmitted, because the acknowledgment does not say which copy it answers. The timestamp
option removes that ambiguity, so the check needs the option to be absent, and it is: the
scenario turns it off.

**How the rule is observed.** The written procedure reads the smoothed value before and
after and requires it to be unchanged. The model gives a sharper observable: a sample is
taken in `TcpBaseAlg::rttMeasurementComplete`, and that function is the only place that
publishes `srtt`, `rttvar` and `rto`. So a forbidden sample is not a changed value, it is a
**publication that must not happen**. The check forbids it outright, which is stronger than
comparing two values: a sample that happened to give the same number would still be a
violation of the rule, and this form catches it.

The guard runs beside the ordered steps. Without that it could not be written at all: a
blocking guard would hold the cursor for its whole window, so the acknowledgment that proves
the exchange completed could not be observed inside it.

**The model conforms.** The run takes exactly one round-trip sample, from the first
segment, and cancels the measurement when the timer expires:

    Measured RTT=0.10804ms, updated SRTT=0.013505ms, new RTO=2625.067525ms
    Performing retransmission #1; increasing RTO from 2.625067525s to 5.25013505s,
        and cancelling RTT measurement

The guard was verified to detect a sample: opened before the permitted one instead of after
it, it fires with "forbidden event occurred at t=0.20010804".

That first line also shows the defect `Rfc6298FirstMeasurement.test` records, on a different
link: the measured trip is 0.108 ms and the smoothed value becomes 0.0135 ms, one eighth of
it, where RFC 6298 section 2.2 requires the two to be equal.

**Why the check is not vacuous.** A forbidden publication is only meaningful if a permitted
one is possible in the same window, so the last step observes the acknowledgment of the
retransmission actually arriving. Without it the guard would hold on a run where nothing
happened at all. The scenario also sends exactly two segments, so no later segment can take
a legitimate sample inside the guard's window and trip it for the wrong reason.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// A pure acknowledgment carries the same port and no SYN, so a step that wants a data
// segment says so.
static bool dataSegment(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr && context.event.packet->getByteLength() > 100;
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc6298_karns_rule")
        // Discard the second data segment once. Its retransmission gets through, and the
        // acknowledgment of that retransmission is the ambiguous one Karn's rule is about.
        .intercept(tap("relay")
                       .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                       .minBytes(100).nth(2)
                       .drop().describe("the second data segment, dropped once"))
        // The first segment is acknowledged cleanly, so the estimator takes its one
        // permitted sample here. This proves the signal is alive, so the guard below
        // forbids something the model really does publish.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("srtt").first()
                  .describe("the one permitted round-trip sample, from the first segment")
                  .within(5.0))
        // Host A gives up on the second segment and sends it again.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).nth(2)
                  .capture("retransmittedSeq", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .describe("the retransmission of the second data segment")
                  .within(20.0))
        // From here on, no sample may be taken. The acknowledgment of the retransmission
        // arrives inside this window, and it must not reach the estimator.
        .meanwhile(never(on("host1.tcp").signal("srtt")
                             .describe("a round-trip sample taken from the retransmission")
                             .within(5.0)))
        // The exchange really did complete inside the guard's window, so the guard held
        // over something rather than over nothing.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.ackNo > {retransmittedSeq}")
                  .describe("the acknowledgment of the retransmitted segment")
                  .within(5.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc6298_karns_rule compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.protocoltest.PacketTap;

// Two hosts with a relay on the link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network KarnNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        relay: PacketTap { @display("p=400,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> relay.a;
        relay.b     <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = KarnNet
sim-time-limit = 40s
seed-set = 0

# static ARP: the only frames on the wire are the TCP/IP ones.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# The timestamp option would remove the ambiguity Karn's rule is about.
**.tcp.timestampSupport = false

*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
# Two segments and no more, so no later segment can take a permitted sample inside the
# guard's window.
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 1000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = -1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSinkApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc6298_karns_rule: PASS
